# general-knowledge-weather.kb - General Knowledge / Weather
# Format: concept | intent | answer | keywords | patterns | tl | bis
# 5 entries, one genuinely distinct fact per line.
#
# "what is the weather today" needs live data and there is none behind
# this bank. The entry says so rather than guessing, which is the
# difference between an honest limit and a confident invention.
monsoon | definition | A monsoon is a seasonal reversal of the wind pattern, and the reversal is the defining feature rather than the rain: the same region gets its wet and dry seasons because the wind changes direction between them. It happens where land and sea heat up at different rates, since land warms and cools faster, and that difference flips the pressure gradient twice a year. The consequence in Asia is the summer monsoon, when moist air from the ocean is drawn inland over a very hot landmass and produces prolonged heavy rain, and the winter monsoon when the direction reverses and the continent sends dry continental air out to sea. The Philippines sits inside this system rather than at its edge, which is why its rainfall is high all year with a pronounced wet season, and why the problem is usually a flood rather than a total lack of water. The same mechanism produces the summer wet season across South Asia, where the timing of the monsoon planting season is a matter of food security for hundreds of millions. | seasonal, wind, wet season, dry season, rain, asia, philippines | what is a monsoon, why does the philippines have a rainy season, what causes monsoon | tag-ulan | monsoon
typhoon | definition | A tropical cyclone is a storm that forms over warm ocean water, and the word typhoon is the name used in the western Pacific, while the same thing is a hurricane in the Atlantic and a cyclone in the Indian Ocean. It needs warm water, unstable air aloft, and a pre-existing disturbance to organise around, and it is the warmth of the sea that supplies the energy as a rule of thumb: the top of a cyclone is roughly as warm as the ocean below it, so when it passes over cooler water it weakens. The structure is a low-pressure centre with bands of rain spiralling inward, and the destructive part is not mainly the wind but the storm surge, a rise in sea level under the storm that floods the coast well inland of where the wind is strongest. That is why a modest storm can be catastrophic in a bay, and why the standard advice in the Philippines is to evacuate early rather than to wait, since the surge arrives ahead of the eye. | cyclone, hurricane, storm surge, eye, rainfall, pacific, warning | what is a typhoon, what is the difference between typhoon and hurricane, what is storm surge | bagyo | typhoon


weather | definition | Weather is the state of the atmosphere at a place over hours or days, and I can explain the system that produces it, but I cannot tell you what it is doing in your town right now. There is no live source behind me, and a made-up temperature is worse than no answer. What I can do is describe how weather works, why forecasts are uncertain, and how to read one properly. | weather, maulan, uulan, umulan, panahon, klima, hain, humidity, ngayon, today, forecast, temperature, storm | what is the weather, what is the weather today, maulan ba ngayon, uulan ba, ano ang panahon | weather | panahon
forecast | definition | A forecast is a statement about what the weather will probably do, and the word probably is doing more work than most people notice. It is produced by simulating the atmosphere with current observations fed in, so the further ahead it goes the more it depends on assumptions that can fail. Skill varies sharply with lead time and with geography: a sea breeze is predictable, a convective storm three hours out is close to a coin toss, which is why forecasts are given as ranges. | forecast, saklaw, prediction, probably, simulate, observation, range, lead time | how are weather forecasts made, how far ahead is a forecast accurate, bakit mali ang forecast | forecast | saklaw
climate | definition | Climate is the weather averaged over decades, which is the whole difference and the reason the two words are used loosely. A cold week is not climate change, and a warm decade is not weather. The metrics are averages and extremes, and both matter, since a heatwave that becomes routine is the more serious signal even though the average has barely moved. This is also why climate is discussed in tens of years while weather is discussed in hours. | climate, weather, average, decade, heatwave, extreme | what is climate, is climate change the same as weather, why does climate matter | climate | klima
storm | definition | A storm is a system of organised wind and rain produced by air rising, and storms are named by type depending on what sustains them. Tropical cyclones need warm ocean water and rotate because of the planet's spin, which is why they do not form near the equator. Thunderstorms are convection on a small scale, and the danger in them is lightning rather than wind, which is why the advice is to get indoors rather than to shelter from the rain. | storm, bagyo, typhoon, wind, alak, rain, cyclone, thunder, lightning | what causes storms, ano ang bagyo, is a storm dangerous | storm | bagyo
humidity | definition | Humidity is the amount of water vapour in air, and the number people quote is usually relative humidity, which is the amount as a percentage of what the air could hold at that temperature. That makes the figure hard to read on its own: 60 per cent feels very different at 15 degrees and at 35. The thing that actually affects people is how quickly sweat evaporates, which is why humid heat feels worse than dry heat at the same temperature. | humidity, vapour, air, relative, percentage, sweat | what is humidity, why does humidity make heat feel worse, what is relative humidity | humidity | basang
